WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.316
Scenario: A person claiming to organize a campus event

2
00:00:03.316 --> 00:00:06.632
sends a form requesting your ID number, date of

3
00:00:06.632 --> 00:00:09.947
birth, home address and bank details to “confirm attendance.”

4
00:00:09.947 --> 00:00:13.263
Safest response: Option A: Verify the organizer through the

5
00:00:13.263 --> 00:00:16.579
official event channel and ask which fields are necessary

6
00:00:16.579 --> 00:00:19.895
before sharing anything. Confirm purpose and legitimacy, then disclose

7
00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:23.211
only the minimum required information using an approved channel.

8
00:00:23.211 --> 00:00:26.526
Option B is unsafe because A plausible event does

9
00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:30.579
not establish the form’s authenticity or justify collecting unrelated sensitive data.

10
00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:33.895
Option C is unsafe because False information can create

11
00:00:33.895 --> 00:00:37.211
operational problems and still expose you to malicious links

12
00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:40.526
or follow-up requests. Option D is unsafe because A

13
00:00:40.526 --> 00:00:43.842
different channel does not fix an unverified request or

14
00:00:43.842 --> 00:00:47.158
excessive data collection. In practice: Contact the event organizer

15
00:00:47.158 --> 00:00:50.474
using a published official channel. Do not submit unnecessary

16
00:00:50.474 --> 00:00:53.789
financial or identity data. Key takeaway: Check purpose, legitimacy

17
00:00:53.789 --> 00:00:56.000
and necessity before disclosing personal information.
